rgin": 18.21, "analyst_adjustment": 0.29, "key_factors": ["Healey's incumbency in a D+14 state with unanimous Solid/Safe D expert ratings", "Stable polling since spring shows a 16-21 point Healey lead", "Trump's national presence weighs on the Republican nominee in Massachusetts", "Minogue's record $6.1 million self-funding has not moved the numbers"], "rationale": "Polls from June through September consistently show Healey ahead by sixteen to twenty-one points, matching the fundamentals of a deeply Democratic state with a sitting governor, and all seven expert ratings place the race in the Solid or Safe Democratic column. Minogue's record self-funding has not dented the lead, and prediction markets price a Healey win at roughly ninety-five percent.", "watch": "A worsening energy-price squeeze or a late Healey scandal, amplified by Minogue's unlimited personal spending, could narrow the margin, though a genuine upset would require a historic shift.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Maura Healey (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Massachusetts Governor Maura Healey seeks a second term against Republican businessman Mike Minogue, with independent Andrea James also on the ballot. The state's Democratic lean sits near fourteen points, and every major race rater places the contest in the Solid or Safe Democratic column. Polling since spring has been remarkably stable: September surveys show Healey ahead by sixteen to twenty-one points, in line with averages near twenty points statewide.\n\nMinogue has spent heavily, including a record $6.1 million in a single month, and campaigns as an outsider beholden to no party, but union criticism of his wealth and spending has not moved the numbers. Healey's October energy-emergency declaration and winter heating relief dominate coverage, and Boston's mayor has endorsed her. Prediction markets price her win chances around ninety-five percent, and the expected margin lands near eighteen points.", "history": [{"date": "2026-